WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:21.070
So far, as we’ve journeyed through the microcosmos,
the organisms we’ve shown you are mostly aquatic.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:28.840
You might find them in oceans or rivers or
lakes, finding their own niche in their watery ecosystem.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:37.820
And surrounding them, dissolved in the water,
is always oxygen, which is essential to their survival.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:44.050
But as you travel down and find where water
meets sediment, a transition occurs.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:50.480
Yes, from more liquid to more solid, but more importantly, the sediments

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.200
impede the movement of oxygen.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:03.710
Which means that, as oxygen gets consumed
by living organisms, the concentration goes down.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.520
The transition here is, in many ways, even
more distinct than the transition from the

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.220
land to the sea in our macro-world.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.720
Yes, it is difficult for fish to live out
of water, and hard for us to live in it, but

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:26.900
ultimately, fish and humans are both based
on roughly the same metabolic chemistry.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.380
We both rely on oxygen.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.271
But here at this transition, where the environment
goes from oxic, meaning there is oxygen, to

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:43.430
anoxic, meaning there isn’t, life’s metabolic
chemistry fundamentally shifts.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.660
It’s muddy there.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:53.080
Slimy, a little smelly, maybe even a little
gross, but to many organisms, the oxic anoxic

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:59.670
transition is a shifting chemical boundary
that has created a challenge for life...a

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:06.170
challenge that changed it’s nature, a challenge
it conquered.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:12.130
So let’s talk about the organisms that prefer
an environment void of oxygen, because many

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:18.680
of them will end up shaping the world around
the organisms that live along this transition.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.400
Now we’re not quite sure, but we believe
this long, large, tightly-coiled bacterium

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.900
is a type of spirochete, many of which prefer
to live without oxygen.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.370
They come up often in these mud samples and
you might know some of their kind as the more

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:42.480
familiar, and less welcome organisms that
cause syphilis and Lyme disease.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:49.650
And just to note, this video is not sped up—the
organism really moves like this.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:56.560
Spirochete’ are uniquely motile, able to
move around even in thick, viscous substances.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:05.129
To vastly oversimplify the entire basis of
our life as we know it, oxygen’s big, important

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:12.989
role is to accept electrons in the chemical
processes that make our cells go and our existence

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.560
possible, so, thanks oxygen.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:23.220
But organisms that live in anoxic environments
substitute oxygen with other chemicals.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:29.769
Some, like this ciliate Caenomorpha, are found
in environments rich in oxygen’s downstairs

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:38.560
neighbor on the periodic table, sulfur, along
with bacteria that thrive in a sulfuric world.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:47.099
Now these look like vibrating, hyperactive
worms, but they’re actually bacteria, specifically

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:52.280
a purple sulfur bacteria, these are Thiospirillum.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:59.100
Inside the Thiospirillum are little globules
where they stock sulfur for later use.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:04.580
They’re photosynthetic and they often prefer
anoxic environments.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:10.080
These bacteria use various forms of sulfur
to produce their energy—including, in the

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.400
case of thiospirillum, those sulfur stores.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:24.159
In these kinds of sulfuric sediments, you’ll
find concentrations of sulfur and oxygen as

00:04:24.159 --> 00:04:26.380
opposing gradients.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:32.560
As you descend into sediment, the concentration
of oxygen decreases, consumed by the life

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.720
that depends on it.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:42.910
Meanwhile, sulfide diffuses upwards from the
anoxic microbes below, and as you travel further

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:48.640
down, the sulfur concentration increases as
you get closer to its source.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.980
But in that sediment is a layer where oxygen and sulfur co-exist.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:04.380
This is the oxic-anoxic interface, a several
100 uM thick boundary that attracts bacteria

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:10.930
like these Achromatium oxaliferum, a large
sulfur bacteria found in freshwater and salt

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:15.590
marsh sediments that comes up often in our
mud samples.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.680
Similar to Thiospirillum, Achromatium has
small sulfur globules.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:26.560
But the bacteria is also able to roll upwards
through the sediment in what seems to be a

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:34.250
response to the presence of oxygen, suggesting
that it might take up oxygen as well.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:40.960
Currents in the water and various other changes
in the environment can affect where the oxic-anoxic

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:48.480
interface is, so Achromatium and other bacteria
that thrive in this layer need the ability

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:56.100
to sense and move in response to the shifting
oxygen and sulfur layers.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:02.610
These gradient roving Achromatium have so
far been difficult to grow in large quantities

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:08.670
in a lab, perhaps because their natural environment
and the relationships they have with their

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.760
surrounding microbes are just too complicated.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:18.130
As a result, these bacteria remain mysterious
giants.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:24.139
Their bodies contain sizable stores of calcite,
but we don’t know what they’re for.

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:28.860
Some scientists have guessed that they might
help control the acidity of the cell, while

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.889
others have suggested it might help regulate
the cell’s buoyancy.

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:39.050
There are many theories, including that the
amount of calcite inside Achromatium makes

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:41.419
them off-putting to predators.

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:46.770
Now, we don’t know if that’s the case
with this Loxodes magnus, which is valiantly

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:50.430
trying and failing to eat this Achromatium.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:56.330
We did observe it for a while, and it kept
rejecting the giant bacterium, so at the very

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:02.310
least, there seems to be something unappetizing
about them.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:09.560
Here with some Achromatium, is a fellow interface
bacteria Macromonas, moving around with the

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.880
use of its flagella.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:17.550
Macromonas seem to also keep a sulfide stock
in little globules, and they’re distinguished

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:24.900
by inclusions where they store calcium oxalate,
which may help the cell keep up its metabolism

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.730
even when the conditions around it aren’t
good.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:34.389
You can see the inclusions in this clip here—the
larger those calcium oxalate stores are, the

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:37.290
slower the bacteria moves.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:44.110
There is, of course, so much more to the story
of mud and microbes.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.230
The oxidations and the reductions.

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:53.289
The various forms of the sulfurs and the oxygens,
not to mention the carbons and nitrogens that…we

00:07:53.289 --> 00:07:55.340
did not even mention.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:00.639
It’s messy, and we don’t mean muddy...we
mean, complicated.

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:04.240
As you might have noticed, oxygen is pretty
important to biochemical mechanisms.

00:08:05.259 --> 00:08:12.419
We can live weeks without food, days without
water, but only moments without oxygen.

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:18.139
And metabolic systems on this planet evolved
largely in the presence of this marvelous,

00:08:18.139 --> 00:08:22.580
versatile, and common electron acceptor.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.330
Without it, everything changes.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.379
Every balance has to be re-weighted.

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:30.539
Every gradient shifted.

00:08:30.539 --> 00:08:37.370
And yet when you step back and look at these
anoxic environments, everything seems to come

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.979
together in a muddy elegance.

00:08:40.979 --> 00:08:45.560
One gradient gives way to another, one process
swaps out for another.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:51.830
And the microcosmos distributes itself accordingly.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:58.040
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.920
